# Pre-mortem light — cli#235 cure
- date: 2026-10-08
- lenses: michael-feathers, tony-hoare, saltzer-schroeder

## Feathers risks
- F1: Characterization test too narrow — only pins hash-matched case; adopter-edited content path untested. Mitigation: test both branches (match -> unchanged; differ -> refused).
- F2: Snapshot drift — if bundle changes (hook decl/undecl) the test count changes. Mitigation: assert zero refused rather than exact count; count-independence.
- F3: Dry-run path divergence — reclassification only fires on real write. Mitigation: skip dry-run (writer never fires there).

## Hoare risks
- H1: hashContent read of outputContent AND existing file — double hash cost. Mitigation: only hash when AlreadyExists fires (exceptional path).
- H2: Readfile may throw on broken symlink — classify as refused rather than unchanged. Mitigation: try/catch around read; fall through to refuse.

## Saltzer-Schroeder risks
- S1: Complete mediation — writeSafely's AlreadyExists check still owns the write-time boundary. The reclassification is read-only post-check. Mitigation: no bypass; reclassify after the throw, not before.
- S2: Adopter-edited user-scope hook content silently treated as "unchanged" if we hash-match wrong. Mitigation: strict byte-for-byte comparison via hashContent.

## Highest-concern folds
- F1 -> test both match AND differ cases
- H2 -> read failure maps to refuse, not unchanged (keep adopter protection)
